## Skin Tone vs Undertone: Why Most Nail Colour Advice Gets It Wrong

Skin tone is what you see when you look at your hand in the mirror, the depth of pigment on the surface, from fair to deep. Undertone is the fixed, subtle hue beneath that surface, and it predicts how a colour will actually behave on your hand far more reliably than skin tone alone.

Most guides collapse these two ideas into one, which is why "fair skin" advice can feel right for one fair-skinned reader and completely wrong for another. Two people can share an identical surface depth and have entirely different undertones, one leaning warm, one leaning cool, and a shade that flatters one will look chalky or sallow on the other.

This is also why "**nail colors for every skin tone**" cannot mean one list per depth category. It has to mean one framework that adjusts for undertone within each depth. Undertone does not change with a tan, with the seasons, or with how much sun you got last weekend. It is the constant. Skin tone is the variable that shifts around it. Once you know your undertone, every recommendation in this guide, and every shade you pick up at a counter, gets filtered through that lens first.

