## How to Find Your Undertone in 60 Seconds (Vein Test, Jewellery Test, and One More)

There are three quick checks, and each one should confirm the others. The vein test: look at the inside of your wrist in natural daylight. Green veins suggest a warm undertone, blue or purple veins suggest cool, and a mix of both suggests neutral. The jewellery test: gold jewellery tends to flatter warm undertones, silver flatters cool, and if both look equally fine on you, that is a strong signal for neutral. The third check is how your skin reacts to sun, warm undertones often tan easily into golden or caramel tones, while cool undertones are more likely to burn before they tan.

The reason to run all three is that any single test can be ambiguous on its own, vein colour can be hard to read under artificial light, and jewellery preference can be influenced by trends rather than true compatibility. When two or three of these checks agree, you have your answer.

This matters more than most people realise because **nail colors for every skin tone** ultimately come down to undertone-matching, not depth-matching. A cool undertone calls for nail shades with pink, blue, or purple bases. A warm undertone calls for shades with yellow, peach, or golden bases. A neutral undertone has the most flexibility, sitting comfortably between the two. Once you have this answer, you can use it for the rest of your life, not just for nail polish, but for foundation, jewellery, and clothing too. For a step-by-step breakdown of the identification process with visual examples, this <a href="https://www.revlon.com/blogs/makeup/how-to-identify-your-skin-undertones">skin undertone identification guide</a> is a useful supporting reference.

