## The Nude Polish Problem: How to Find Your Actual Match Across Every Skin Tone

The nude that looked perfect in the bottle and wrong on the nail is the single most common complaint across every skin tone in this guide, and the cause is almost always the same. The nude is the wrong temperature for your undertone, not the wrong shade of nude.

A pink-based nude on a warm undertone will read as slightly grey or "too pink," exactly the complaints that show up again and again in reader feedback. A yellow or peach-based nude on a cool undertone will read as sallow or muddy, even if the depth is correct for the skin tone. This is why "nude" cannot be treated as a single category, it is really three categories, cool-based, warm-based, and neutral-based, and only one of those three will sit correctly on any given hand.

The practical fix is to stop shopping for nude by depth (fair nude, medium nude, deep nude) and start shopping by undertone within your depth range. A warm undertone on fair skin needs a peachy or golden-beige nude, while a cool undertone on the same depth needs a pink-based or rosy nude. <strong>Nail colors for every skin tone</strong> only solves the nude problem when the undertone filter is applied before the depth filter, not after.

