## Finish Matters More Than You Think: Crème, Metallic, and Jelly Across Skin Tones

You have been choosing the wrong variable. Colour gets all the attention, but finish changes how that colour behaves on your specific skin just as much as undertone does, and almost nobody talks about it.

A jelly nail formula is a translucent polish that allows the natural nail and skin colour to show through, which means the same jelly red can look rosy on fair skin, brick-toned on medium skin, and almost black-cherry on deep skin, all from one bottle. This is not a flaw in the formula, it is the formula responding to what is beneath it.

Crème finishes are the most predictable across skin tones because their opacity means the colour you see in the bottle is close to the colour you will see on the nail, which makes crème the safest starting point if you are still building confidence in a new shade family.

Metallics and shimmers add reflectivity, and reflectivity interacts with the warmth or coolness of your skin's surface. A gold metallic tends to amplify warmth, which is part of why it performs so well on warm undertones and golden-toned deep skin, while silver and cool-toned chrome finishes do the same for cool undertones. If you wear gel specifically, the formula itself shifts these interactions slightly further, and <a href="/blog/skin-tone/gel-nail-colors-for-skin-tone">gel nail colors for your skin tone</a> covers formula-specific guidance for exactly this. For a professional framing of how finish and undertone interact, this <a href="https://www.nsinails.co.uk/blogs/nail-knowledge/how-to-choose-nail-colours-for-different-skin-tones">nail industry guidance on undertone matching</a> is worth a look.

