## The One Rule That Replaces Every Shade List

Forget the shade list. The single rule that changes everything is this: undertone decides compatibility, depth decides intensity, and finish decides how loudly the colour speaks. Every recommendation in this guide, and every recommendation you will ever need at a salon counter, runs through those three filters in that order.

This is also the rule that makes "universally flattering" colours make sense for the first time. Red, navy, and nude are not universal because every version of them works on everyone, they are universal because each of those families contains a warm version and a cool version, and one of those versions will always align with your undertone. The myth was never that these colours fail, it was that nobody explained which version of them to reach for.

You now have the framework that most guides skip entirely. Use it at your next appointment, use it the next time a trending shade tempts you, and use it to finally explain to yourself why some colours have always looked "expensive" on your hands and others never quite landed. That is the difference undertone makes, and it is the only difference that was ever really at play.
